---
import Project from "../../layouts/Project.astro";
---

<Project
  title="LearnFlow"
  kind="chrome extension · ai learning tool"
  description="A Chrome extension that explains unfamiliar terms in context while you read."
  lead="LearnFlow keeps learning in motion: select any unfamiliar term on a page and get an instant AI-powered explanation right where you are, without opening a new tab or losing your place."
  links={[
    { label: "Source code →", href: "https://github.com/keeeeeliu/LearnFlow", primary: true },
  ]}
  media={{
    kind: "image",
    src: "/project_gifs/learnflow1.gif",
    alt: "LearnFlow browser extension demo showing an in-page explanation flow",
  }}
  facts={[
    { label: "Role", value: "Builder" },
    { label: "Type", value: "Chrome extension" },
    { label: "Focus", value: "In-context learning" },
    { label: "For", value: "Students & developers" },
  ]}
  stack={["Chrome Extension", "JavaScript", "AI", "HTML", "CSS"]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      You're deep into reading a technical article. The concepts are clicking. Your mind is racing.
      Then you hit it: binary search tree. Wait, what's that again?
    </p>
    <p>
      You open a new tab, Google it, skim Stack Overflow, maybe watch a video. Ten minutes later,
      you've lost your place, your train of thought, and your momentum.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>
      Learning shouldn't break your flow. LearnFlow lets you select any unfamiliar term and get a
      quick explanation in context, so reading and understanding can stay part of the same motion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Turns selected text into an instant AI-powered explanation</li>
      <li>Keeps the explanation on the current page instead of sending you into tab-switching mode</li>
      <li>Supports technical reading for students practicing LeetCode or developers exploring new stacks</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      The small interruptions are what make hard reading exhausting. LearnFlow is built around a simple
      belief: curiosity should be answered at the speed it appears, without making you abandon the page
      that sparked it.
    </p>
  </section>
</Project>
